pr_comments_excerpt:
@abyrd: The need to run R5 on Java 8 is starting to cause some friction. Today after loading a very large GTFS I got `java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: org.opengis.referencing.datum.DatumFactory is not an ImageIO SPI class` because I happened to be using a Java 11 runtime. Current R5 `dev` branch cannot run on Java 9+, primarily due to the Geotools version. We should try to get everything working on Java 11+ by the end of 2019.

This means updating gtfs-lib and osm-lib to run on Java 11. I contacted @evansiroky and @landonreed about this, hopefully we can coordinate on harmonizing the dependency versions across all these libraries.
---
@abyrd: I have the backend using a locked snapshot build of this right now and it seems to be working. 
---
@abyrd: There's a problem with updating JTS in gtfs-lib and R5: we have large amounts of serialized TransportNetworks and GTFS feeds (in MapDBs) that expect all JTS objects to be in package `com.vividsolutions` instead of `org.locationtech`. This breaks all deserialization, we'd have to rebuild all the TransportNetworks and GTFS MapDBs from scratch, which could be done but is not the most straightforward thing.

However, since this won't run on Java 11 without migrating to newer GeoTools (and therefore newer JTS) I'm not sure we have much choice in the long term but to re-process all the GTFS and rebuild every network.

Another problem is that in TransportNetworkCache we build R5 networks directly from gtfsCache, which is loading them directly from cached MapDBs. Once we migrated all the MapDBs, older versions of R5 could no longer build Networks from those MapDBs. Maybe the solution is to make a new suffix for the Java11 mapdbs, like `.v2.mapdb` so newer R5 workers and newer backends will use those files while leaving the old ones alone. There may be a large number of different places where various pieces of code generate the names of the GTFS MapDB files though, so that could get messy.
---
@abyrd: I have confirmed that updating gtfs-lib to use new suffixes `.v2.db` and `.v2.db.p` may be straightforward. The only places I see these suffixes in the entire project and dependencies is gtfs-lib file BaseGTFSCache. A quick search and replace there, replacing `.db` with `.v2.db` causes all databases to be rebuilt and they should be uploaded to S3 under these new names, leaving the old ones in tact. We'd want to do a migration just to reduce lag the first time people use a feed, which should involve getting all recently used feed IDs from Mongo and hitting the GraphQL API to get something like all the route names from each feed, one by one in a loop.
---
@abyrd: We're back to using the same old GTFS MapDB files, thanks to an update to gtfs-lib. This seems to be working, ready for review. Note that a large number of changes can be glossed over: all the deletions from src/main/java/com/conveyal/r5/otp2/ are removing unused OTP2 code from R5.

