pr_body:
We added support for `@Bind` recognizing parameter names, now let's go even further and ditch the annotation if you don't actually need it.

Open question: should we just get rid of the default "name by parameter index" entirely?  This wouldn't prevent people from using positional parameters in fluent queries, but it would mean

```
@SqlUpdate("insert into table values(:0)")
void doSomething(int param);
```

would need to be rewritten to

```
@SqlUpdate("insert into table values(:param)")
void doSomething(int param);
```

The only reason I can see this being used would be if your code does not have parameter names enabled (which is a javac flag IIRC) but then you might as well just `@Bind("param")` instead... Thoughts?

pr_comments_excerpt:
@qualidafial: Do we really want to drop positional parameter support? We're sure there aren't enough users that use it to justify keeping it?

I'm also not sure I like falling back to positional parameters behavior depending on the user's compiler flags. IMO it should only work (or not) depending on compiler flags, not change behavior.

---
@stevenschlansker: To clarify, I wasn't suggesting removing positional parameter support.  There was a bizarre mode where `@Bind` would create a _named_ parameter with the stringified position index as a name.  So it's a named positional parameter.  Confusing!

Now that's gone.  I simplified the rules a lot:

It's always bound as a positional parameter, which will only be used if the name doesn't match.
If you omit the name, you _must_ have the parameter name available, otherwise it's an error.

Think that's reasonable?

---
@qualidafial: Consider comments, then :shipit: when ready.

---
@stevenschlansker: I think we should rethink how binding happens entirely.  I've dropped a note to that effect on the CHANGES_FOR_3.0 document and we'll revisit it later.  I'm going to merge this partially as a WIP.

